# Security & PII Rules

## No secrets in code or config files
Never commit passwords, API keys, Vault tokens, or AWS credentials to source control.
Always read from Vault (via Spring Cloud Vault) or AWS Secrets Manager at startup.

```java
// BAD
String dbPassword = "mypassword123";

// GOOD — injected from Vault
@Value("${spring.datasource.password}")
private String dbPassword;
```

## PII column encryption
The following fields are PII and must be encrypted at rest using Vault transit (envelope encryption):
`work_email`, `work_phone`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `date_of_birth`.

Use a `@Convert(converter = EncryptedStringConverter.class)` JPA converter — do not roll custom encryption inline.

## Consent check before risk profiling
Before executing risk assessment for an employee, check the `risk_profiling_opt_out` flag from the employee profile or the Consent Service cache.

```java
if (employee.isRiskProfilingOptOut()) {
    auditService.logSuppression(tenantId, employeeId, "risk_profiling_opt_out");
    return; // skip — do not classify as at-risk
}
```

## Human-review gate before CRITICAL/HIGH notification
Never dispatch an employee notification for CRITICAL or HIGH risk without a confirmed `RISK_REVIEWS` record. The notification service checks for this; the risk engine must not bypass it.

## Erasure-aware queries
Any query that reads employee PII must check `employees.erasure_status != 'COMPLETED'` or use a view that excludes erased employees. Erased employees have tokenised PII fields.

## mTLS between services
Service-to-service calls go through Istio service mesh (mTLS STRICT). Do not add HTTP clients that bypass the mesh by calling pod IPs directly.

## Audit logging
Every state-changing operation must publish an event to the Audit Service via Kafka. Include `actor_id`, `actor_role`, `resource_type`, `resource_id`, `before_state`, `after_state`.
